## This campaign's non-negotiables (on top of the integrity core above)

**Frozen evaluation.** The locked tier here is `{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}` and its scorer, the data, and known
ground truth. Unlocked hyperparameters are legitimate levers; "frozen" means the locked tier.

### HARD GATES (do not skip — these are the ones that get skipped under time pressure)
1. **No promotion without independent verification.** Promotion means updating the current-best
   (`RECOMMENDED.json`), marking a scoreboard row `verified: CONFIRMED` / `NEW BEST`, or promoting a
   system-level finding to the project ledger (`../../DISCOVERIES.md`). Before any of those writes you
   must have run `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` for that finding and gotten CONFIRMED. It spawns an independent
   refuter sub-agent: a fresh agent, different or peer model, that never saw your reasoning. Quote the
   verdict line in the logbook entry. A finding that is PROVISIONAL / QUESTIONABLE / INCONCLUSIVE does
   not graduate. A PreToolUse hook enforces this, subject-matched: a promotion write is blocked until
   `results/verdicts/` holds a CONFIRMED verdict **for that experiment** — in supervised runs the
   driver's per-block verification writes it; run `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` yourself where it has not.
   Settled findings are RECORDED in
   `results/JOURNAL.md` §Findings, each citing its verdict artifact by name — a claim with no verdict
   citation is an open thread, not a finding, and steers nothing.
2. **Name the levers each experiment moved, and weigh what moving several together costs.** Moving one
   makes the cause plain. Moving several buys speed and turns the result into a claim about the
   combination: `{{VERIFY_SKILL}}` then grades why it won as QUESTIONABLE (the score still stands), and a
   multi-lever loss kills only the combination, clearing no member lever. Either is legitimate.
   Leaving it unrecorded is not: nothing downstream can recover which knob did the work.
3. **Clear this campaign's own resolution.** A win must exceed the noise, not sit within it. What "the
   noise" means is set once, in `configs/loop.json` `campaign.noise_model`: a `stochastic` campaign
   compares the delta against the replicate spread. Report `Δ ± spread`. A `deterministic` one compares
   it against a numerical `tolerance`, because repeated runs are identical and there is no spread to
   clear. The runner records the verdict in `results/checks_<NN>.json`.
4. **A result is not saved until it has a scoreboard row, a `result_<NN>.json`, its figure, and its
   snapshot.**
5. **The loop does not end when an experiment does.** Step 12 goes back to step 1; a completed experiment
   is not a stopping cue. The loop ends only on the human's `results/STOP` or a recorded
   `results/stop_request.json` (reason code and evidence; `exhausted` must name zero untried levers).
   Stopping is a decision you record, not a turn that happens to end. A Stop hook enforces this: it
   refuses an unearned end-of-turn and hands you the JOURNAL NEXT pointer. Record the stop. Those two
   exits are the whole contract; this gate is where it lives. Supervisor mode: they govern the driver's
   campaign level; a worker's turn ends legitimately when its unit's iteration contract validates, and
   the Stop hook stands down for driver-spawned workers. The **stall release**, the hook letting a session
   go after ~25 continuations with no new scoreboard row, is not a third way out. It is a report that the
   loop is alive and producing nothing. Diagnose it rather than riding to it; `roles/worker.md` has the
   procedure.

## Where things live (this campaign's map; skills route through this table, not a hardcoded path)

Where a kit skill needs a campaign document, it is told to consult this table rather than assume a path,
so a campaign that renames or relocates one corrects the row here and those skills follow. Skills still
name fixed per-run artifacts (`results/result_<NN>.json`, `results/checks_<NN>.json`,
`results/queue.json`, …) inline; this table covers the documents whose location varies between campaigns,
not every path in the tree.

The rows below are the standard layout. Correct any that this campaign does not match, and do it before
the first experiment. A pre-restructure tree keeps the whole contract in one `docs/charter.md` (lint-ok:
names the retired layout on purpose) and has no `campaign.md` or `roles/`; a campaign using the retired
multi-stage suffix has `docs/charter_<NAME>.md`, `configs_<NAME>/`, `results_<NAME>/`; the oldest trees
call the campaign definition `docs/program.md` (lint-ok: names the retired layout on purpose). A row
naming a file this campaign does not have is worse than no row, because an agent will trust it.

